an 10 vaccination centers in  vienna  here you can get your first second or  third jab without needing an appointment  and suddenly more people are keen to do  so  in austria about 64 percent of the  population are fully vaccinated that's  one of the lowest rates in western  europe and it's not high enough to stop  the surge  a lot of people need to be vaccinated  now for their everyday lives for their  job or for private things to go out for  a meal in the evening to go to the  cinema and that's why we're seeing a  significant increase in vaccinations  but most of the people here have come  for a second or third jab only around 20  percent are getting their first shot and  that percentage has to increase experts  say to turn the tide  dr catherine schmitz is a behavioral  economist and psychologist at the  cluster of excellence the politics of  inequality at constance university and  the tour gao institute of economics and  she joins us now good to have you with  us i know that you studied people's  attitude towards getting vaccinated  already since the beginning of the  pandemic and you found that compulsory  vaccination actually reduces people's  willingness to get vaccinated how come  why is that  yes it's important to notice that we  studied people's agreement with being  like united not very compliant so we ask  whether people are okay with being like  me that you get vaccinated because you  are forced to but you don't want to and  this is what we what we study  and why are people more happy being  vaccinated if it is voluntary than  enforced well two important reasons is  the lack of autonomy people strive for  autonomy we know everyone who has  children has this experience and yeah  and mandates restrict autonomy and the  other  uh reason is moral dis engagement so  people feel morally obliged to do the  right thing to get vaccinated for  example but if the government takes this  responsibility this crowds out the  feeling of moral responsibility  now we could say well we are  okay but we've been we've been we've  been in in situations similar situations  before uh and there was compulsory  vaccination when it came to smallpox uh  that was uh accepted in in west germany  right up until to  1976. so why is it a no-go now when it  comes to kovit-19  so the vacuum is new there is a new  method the mrna method so people are  unsure about long-term effects that's a  different situation but maybe um with  the smallpox it mainly hurts children  and with covet mainly older people are  at risk  risk and in germany we have the not so  nice situation that  older people are not so well integrated  in the families and maybe yeah people  would care more about their children  about than about the elderly  of course there's this this big  discussion about the legality now in the  year 2021 when it comes to  making vaccination compulsory uh if we  just take a quick look  at the most quoted paragraph in german  law for our viewers to better understand  because the basic law for the federal  republic of germany article 2 says one  everyone has the right to the free  development of his personality provided  that he does not infringe on the rights  of others and i too everyone has the  right to life and physical integrity the  freedom of the person is inviolable  it goes on then but basically the  problem is that that  it gives to it gives both sides  the argument that they need saying it's  my personal decision  or saying  no it's not your personal decision  because unvaccinated people are a danger  to others  that's a dilemma how likely is it that  people who are not vaccinated  can still be convinced  so according to our data it is quite  likely we find that on less than five  percent are consistently opposed to  voluntary vaccination over all the  pandemic all the others changed their  mind so there is a lot of dynamics going  on in vaccination attitudes by the way  this hardcore of resistance is much  larger in case of enforcement but