y  uh we need some definitions first  covid19 is currently a pandemic an  infectious disease  affecting people on a worldwide scale  and as we've learned yet again here in  europe in the last few weeks  with very unpredictable fluctuations  that we call waves  with endemic diseases  numbers of people being infected and  landing in hospitals are fairly constant  in populations over time enough so  that we can at least ballpark predict  patterns of transmission like we can for  instance with seasonal flu  it's an endemic disease that we know  will generally affect more people in the  wintertime and we can broadly forecast  how many which  helps us to manage it to some extent  no one really knows yet how long it'll  take covet 19 to reach that status  because it'll depend on a range of  factors the number one factor  is how many people acquire at least some  immunity to it which involves  either getting infected by the virus or  vaccinated against it and how long lived  that protection proves to be  is another big part of the puzzle um  vaccine distribution and take-up  varies widely in countries across the  world so experts say  that pandemic numbers won't stabilize  everywhere at the same time but  at some point in the future  after enough people acquire some immune  protection to source kobe 2 through  infection  or vaccination  and barring the appearance of aggressive  new variants of course  researchers think  that covid19 will begin to affect  more predictable numbers of us every  year  at somewhat predictable intervals um  like with seasonal flu which strikes  hardest in winter  when people spend a lot more time  indoors  [Music]  and a quick look to latin america where  military doctors and nurses protested in  peru against where they allege a  dangerous conditions that are leaving  them vulnerable to kobe 19. they rallied  in the capital lima the protesters say  the government isn't giving them  sufficient protective equipment or  medical facilities they say they're  forced to treat covet patients alongside  other patients increasing the risk of  cross-infection  that's all for this edition of our kobit  19 special from me and the team as  always thanks for watching stay safe  stay healthy

